## Explanation
A `char` array is a collection of individual `char`  character  variables stored in contiguous memory locations. While you can use a `char` array to store a sequence of single characters, its most common and powerful application in C is to represent **strings**. C strings are essentially `char` arrays that are terminated by a special null character, `\0`, which signals the end of the string. When you declare a `char` array, you must define its fixed size, ensuring it's large enough to hold all your characters plus the mandatory null terminator.

You can declare a `char` array by specifying its size, like `char greeting[20];`, or by initializing it directly with a string literal, like `char name[] = "Student";`, in which case the compiler automatically calculates the size and adds the null terminator for you. Accessing individual characters within the array is done using zero-indexing, where the first character is at index `0`.

## Visual Model
```mermaid
graph TD
    A[char name[] = "Student";] --> B{Memory Allocation};
    B --> C0[name[0]: 'S'];
    B --> C1[name[1]: 't'];
    B --> C2[name[2]: 'u'];
    B --> C3[name[3]: 'd'];
    B --> C4[name[4]: 'e'];
    B --> C5[name[5]: 'n'];
    B --> C6[name[6]: 't'];
    B --> C7[name[7]: '\0' 'Null Terminator'];
    C0 --- C1 --- C2 --- C3 --- C4 --- C5 --- C6 --- C7;
    C7 --> D{String End};
```

## Example from Class
The `demo_strings.c` file provides excellent examples of creating `char` arrays:

```c
    // string1 is initialized with text. C automatically adds '\0' at the end.
    char name[] = "Student";

    // string2 must be large enough to hold the text + the null terminator
    char greeting[20] = "Hello";
```

In the `name` example, the compiler automatically determines the size needed for "Student" plus the null terminator. In the `greeting` example, we explicitly provide a size of 20, which is more than enough for "Hello" and its null terminator, allowing for future modifications. The `demo_basics.c` file also shows a single `char` variable:

```c
    char section = 'A';
```
This `section` variable holds only one character, unlike a `char` array which holds multiple.

