typedef is a C/C++ keyword that creates an alias (new name) for an existing type. It doesn’t make a new type; it just provides a clearer or shorter name.

Example:
- typedef unsigned long ulong;
- typedef int (*cmp_fn)(const void*, const void*);
- typedef struct Node { int data; struct Node* next; } Node;

Used for readability, portability, and simplifying complex declarations. In C++, the modern equivalent is using Alias = ExistingType;.

